\
Scale Factor - What is it?\
\
The Scale Factor is nothing more than a number we multiply the measured oar bend to obtain force.  Because our system is linear, this single scale factor accounts for the stiffness of the oar, the distance between the sensors and their location about the pin etc. It\'92s important to note that changing the scale factor will only change the values related to your effort: Force, Work, Power (and pSplit which is derived from power).  It won\'92t change the shape of the force curve, ratio, the angles of how much water you are missing at the catch or finish, percent stroke length at 50% work, percent stroke length at max bend etc.  Go here for guidance on how to set the scale factor.\
\
\
strokeLength\
Stroke Length is arc length measured from where 5% of max bend is first achieved in the oar at the beginning of the stroke to where it has reduced to 5% at the end of the stroke.  Stroke length does not include the missed portion at the beginning or end of the stroke (CatchMiss and FinishMiss).\
